<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhian Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhian Singh was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> noble who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> (chief minister) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> in 1839, he and his brothers were leading figures among the factions of the Lahore Darbar, and he was killed in 1843 amid the violent succession struggles that weakened the state before the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhian-singh.jpg" alt="Dhian Singh" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Dhian_Singh,_Sikh_school,_India,_c._mid_1800s_AD,_opaque_watercolor_and_gold_on_paper_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo,_Japan_-_DSC08818.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raja Dhian Singh was born on 22 August 1796. He was a brother of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>, who later founded the Dogra dynasty of the princely state of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suchet-singh">Suchet Singh</a>, who also served the empire; the three were known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> brothers. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> in 1818 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> and held the office for twenty-five years, longer than any other wazir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> died on 27 June 1839 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharak-singh">Kharak Singh</a> was crowned on 1 September, Dhian launched a palace coup on 8 October 1839 in which Chet Singh Bajwa, the emperor's favourite courtier, was killed. Having spread a rumour that Kharak Singh meant to sell the empire's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the British East India Company, he imprisoned him; the emperor later died of slow poisoning by lead and mercury.</p><p>Dhian then placed Kharak's eighteen-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nau-nihal-singh">Nau Nihal Singh</a> on the throne. On 5 November 1840, the day of his father's funeral, Nau Nihal was struck by a collapsing stone gate at Lahore Fort, which also killed Udham Singh, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>. Dhian had him carried inside and barred visitors, even his mother Chand Kaur; early eyewitnesses reported only minor injuries, but he was later pronounced dead with his head smashed. Alexander Gardner noted that of the five artillerymen who carried him, two died mysteriously, two never returned from leave and one disappeared.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a> moved against Chand Kaur on 13 January 1841, Dhian negotiated the ceasefire that led to her abdication and Sher's accession. Chand Kaur was killed in her palace on 11 June 1842 by servants Dhian had put in place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 15 September 1843 Dhian Singh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a> were both killed in a plot led by Ajit Singh Sandhawalia; Dhian was shot and his body cut into pieces. His son Hira Singh killed the assassins the next day and on 17 September became prime minister at 24, with the five-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duleep-singh">Duleep Singh</a> crowned emperor. Hira Singh was killed in a coup on 21 December 1844, a year before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> broke out. A descendant of Dhian, Jagat Dev Singh of the Poonch ruling family, held the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> in 1925 and 1926.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhian Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fakir Azizuddin, a Muslim, served as Ranjit Singh's principal foreign minister and envoy to the British; the Dogra <b>Dhian Singh</b> rose to become chief minister; and finance was managed successively by officials such as Diwan Bhawani Das, Diwan Ganga Ram and Diwan Dina Nath.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1850 Maharaja Gulab Singh granted Poonch to his nephew Moti Singh, son of the Lahore minister <b>Dhian Singh</b>, as a jagir under Jammu and Kashmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/poonch-state">Poonch State</a></li></ul></section>
